WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
 [MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.080
 As you can see, creative innovation

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.600
 spans across curriculum areas and disciplines.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:12.920
 A creative innovator is someone with the ability

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.840
 to spot friction quickly, treat ideas like experiments,

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.920
 and move fast enough to learn on the go.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:23.040
 Creativity is essential because real-world problems are messy

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.320
 and lack instruction manuals.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:29.480
 My school has fostered us by consistently encouraging me

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:33.880
 in challenging environments, such as AP Chem, AP Physics II,

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.960
 and AP Calc BC, where if labs failed

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.560
 or analytic methods broke down, I

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:42.360
 learned to quickly switch to alternative procedures,

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:46.060
 such as using simulations, trying numerical methods,

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:48.280
 or redesigning experiments.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:51.700
 Our district prioritizes creativity and critical thinking

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:54.000
 by assigning real-world projects.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.600
 Students explore subjects they're interested in

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:59.960
 through frequent showcases and see the real-world

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:02.960
 implications of classroom topics.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.800
 As part of the curriculum, students

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.320
 have the opportunity to earn credits for internships.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:10.980
 This past year, I was part of a summer internship

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:14.080
 at the University at Buffalo for six weeks,

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.560
 where I worked on a project called Solaris,

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.600
 a project designed to analyze solar output

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:22.240
 at different altitudes.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:25.600
 Our abstract was selected at NASA for presentation

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:29.760
 at the SmallSat conference and held at Kennedy Space Center,

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:30.760
 Florida.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:33.260
 It was truly a transformative experience,

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:37.240
 where ingenuity and innovation were nationally recognized

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:40.740
 by the lead federal agency responsible for research

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.680
 in aeronautics and space exploration.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.480
 I've learned these skills outside of the classroom,

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:49.340
 too, through involvement in extracurricular activities,

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:52.780
 such as the Technology Student Association and the Science

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:53.760
 Olympiad.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:57.120
 These experiences have taught me how to apply broad scientific

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.040
 knowledge, think critically, and troubleshoot on the fly.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:04.760
 Creative innovation lends itself to critical thinking,

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:08.240
 allowing someone who recognizes uncertainty and bias

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.360
 in their own and others' thoughts

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:14.580
 and weighs ethical consequences before deciding on an outcome.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:18.280
 Simply put, a critical thinker is someone with matter

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:21.080
 behind their curiosity.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.120
 [MUSIC PLAYING]

